There are several ways to center a `div` horizontally in CSS. Here are the most common and effective methods:

## 1. **Using `margin: auto` (Most Common)**

```css
.centered-div {
  width: 50%; /* Must have a width */
  margin: 0 auto;
}
```

This works when the element has a defined width and is a block-level element.

## 2. **Using Flexbox (Modern & Recommended)**

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}

.centered-div {
  /* No width needed, but can be set if desired */
}
```

## 3. **Using CSS Grid**

```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center; /* Centers both horizontally and vertically */
  /* OR */
  justify-items: center; /* Only horizontal centering */
}

/* Or with grid-template-columns: */
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr;
}

.centered-div {
  grid-column: 2;
}
```

## 4. **Using `text-align` (For inline elements)**

```css
.container {
  text-align: center;
}

.centered-div {
  display: inline-block;
}
```

## 5. **Using `position` and `transform` (Advanced)**

```css
.centered-div {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translateX(-50%);
}
```

## **Recommended Approach**

For most cases, **Flexbox** is the best choice because it's:
- Modern and widely supported
- Easy to understand
- Flexible (also handles vertical centering easily)
- Doesn't require knowing the element's width

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}

.centered-div {
  /* Your div styles here */
  width: 300px;
}
```

## Example HTML:
```html
<div class="container">
  <div class="centered-div">
    This div is centered!
  </div>
</div>
```

**Note:** The `margin: auto` method is the simplest for basic centering, but Flexbox is generally preferred for modern layouts.

